    {
      "title": "Diphthongs and triphthongs",
      "html": "<p>A <b>diphthong</b> is a vowel sound that glides from one vowel quality to another in the same syllable. RP English has 8 diphthongs:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>/e\u026a/ \u2014 <i>day, name, wait</i></li>\n    <li>/a\u026a/ \u2014 <i>my, high, buy</i></li>\n    <li>/\u0254\u026a/ \u2014 <i>boy, toy, coin</i></li>\n    <li>/\u0259\u028a/ \u2014 <i>go, boat, no</i></li>\n    <li>/a\u028a/ \u2014 <i>now, house, cow</i></li>\n    <li>/\u026a\u0259/ \u2014 <i>near, here, dear</i></li>\n    <li>/e\u0259/ \u2014 <i>hair, care, there</i></li>\n    <li>/\u028a\u0259/ \u2014 <i>tour, poor</i></li>\n  </ul>\n  <p>A <b>triphthong</b> glides through three vowel qualities in a single syllable: <i>fire</i> /fa\u026a\u0259/, <i>hour</i> /a\u028a\u0259/, <i>player</i> /ple\u026a\u0259/, <i>royal</i> /r\u0254\u026a\u0259l/, <i>lower</i> /l\u0259\u028a\u0259/.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Vowel glide.</li><li><i>Day</i> uses /e\u026a/.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> A diphthong is:<br/><b>A:</b> a vowel that glides between two qualities</p><p><b>Q:</b> Which word contains /e\u026a/?<br/><b>A:</b> day</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "State the diphthong in <i>coin</i>.",
          "a": "/\u0254\u026a/."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Consonant clusters",
      "html": "<p>A <b>consonant cluster</b> is a group of two or more consonants next to each other without a vowel between them, at the beginning or end of a syllable.</p>\n  <h4>Initial clusters</h4>\n  <ul><li>2-consonant: <i>play, snow, cry, glass, twin, dwell</i>.</li>\n  <li>3-consonant: <i>splash, string, scream, spread</i> \u2014 always begins with /s/, then /p, t, k/, then /l, r, w, j/.</li></ul>\n  <h4>Final clusters</h4>\n  <ul><li>2 consonants: <i>help, hand, jump, best</i>.</li>\n  <li>3 consonants: <i>text /tekst/, next /nekst/, sixths /s\u026aks\u03b8s/</i>.</li></ul><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>/spl/ \u2014 three-consonant cluster.</li><li>/sn/ in <i>snake</i>.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Initial cluster in <i>splash</i>:<br/><b>A:</b> /spl/</p><p><b>Q:</b> Which word has a two-consonant initial cluster?<br/><b>A:</b> snake</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Identify the initial cluster in <i>spring</i>.",
          "a": "/spr/."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Intonation and emphatic stress",
      "html": "<p><b>Intonation</b> is the rise and fall of the voice in speech. Two main tunes:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>Falling tune (\u21d8)</b> \u2014 used for statements, commands, WH-questions, exclamations: <i>I am tired.\u21d8 Where is he?\u21d8</i></li>\n    <li><b>Rising tune (\u21d7)</b> \u2014 used for yes/no questions, polite requests, incomplete lists: <i>Are you coming?\u21d7 Would you like tea?\u21d7</i></li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><b>Emphatic (or contrastive) stress</b> puts extra stress on a particular word to change the meaning of a sentence:</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b>I</b> gave her the book \u2014 <i>(I did, not somebody else)</i></li>\n    <li>I gave her the <b>book</b> \u2014 <i>(not the pen)</i></li>\n    <li>I gave <b>her</b> the book \u2014 <i>(not him)</i></li>\n  </ul><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Contrast with someone else.</li><li>Contrast with another recipient.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Emphatic stress on <b>I</b> in <i>I gave her the book</i> means:<br/><b>A:</b> not you</p><p><b>Q:</b> Emphatic stress on <b>her</b> in <i>I gave her the book</i> means:<br/><b>A:</b> not him</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Which tune is used for a yes/no question?",
          "a": "Rising tune (\u21d7)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Rising and falling intonation",
      "html": "<p><b>Rising and falling intonation</b> is a key part of Oral English (SSS 2, English). Master the meaning, the main points and how it is tested in JAMB and WAEC questions on this topic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Intonation is the rise and fall of the voice in speech.</li><li>Two main tunes: Falling tune (\u21d8) \u2014 used for statements, commands, WH-questions, exclamations: I am tired.\u21d8 Where is he?\u21d8 Rising tune (\u21d7) \u2014 used for yes/no questions, polite requests, incomplete lists: Are you coming?\u21d7 Would you like tea?\u21d7 Emphatic (or contrastive) stress puts extra stress on a particular word to change the meaning of a sentence: I gave her the book \u2014 (I did, not somebody else) I gave her the book \u2014 (not the pen) I gave her the book \u2014 (not him) Key points Contrast with someone else.</li><li>Rising tune for politeness.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Falling tune is used for:<br/><b>A:</b> statements \u2014 Statements fall.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Rising tune is used for:<br/><b>A:</b> yes/no questions \u2014 Yes/no questions rise.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Falling tune is used for:",
          "a": "statements \u2014 Statements fall."
        },
        {
          "q": "Rising tune is used for:",
          "a": "yes/no questions \u2014 Yes/no questions rise."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Revision and exam focus",
      "html": "<p>Revise the whole topic by working through its key points in order, then test yourself with the 50-question quiz for this topic. Pay attention to definitions, differences between similar terms, and questions that ask you to apply the topic rather than only recall it.</p><h4>Worked examples</h4><p><b>Q:</b> A diphthong is:<br/><b>A:</b> a vowel that glides between two qualities \u2014 Vowel glide.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Which word contains /e\u026a/?<br/><b>A:</b> day \u2014 <i>Day</i> uses /e\u026a/.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Which word contains /a\u026a/?<br/><b>A:</b> my \u2014 <i>My</i> uses /a\u026a/.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "A diphthong is:",
          "a": "a vowel that glides between two qualities"
        },
        {
          "q": "Which word contains /e\u026a/?",
          "a": "day"
        }
      ]
    }
